module bindings.javascript.redirectFollowCredentialHeaders;

// A client that follows a 3xx itself decides what the next request carries, and
// the WHATWG Fetch standard's HTTP-redirect fetch is explicit about the part
// that cannot simply be copied: when the redirect target's origin differs from
// the current one, the CORS non-wildcard request-header names -- `Authorization`
// first among them -- are deleted from the request's header list, and every
// mainstream client extends that to the rest of the credential set (`Cookie`,
// `Cookie2`, `WWW-Authenticate`, `Proxy-Authorization`). The reason is that the
// 3xx is written by the server the first request reached: it names the next host,
// so a copy of the header set hands that server's operator whatever bearer token,
// session cookie or basic-auth credential the caller attached for the original
// host. That is CWE-200 with no injection anywhere in it -- the URL the client
// fetches is exactly the one it was told to fetch.
//
// The fact recorded here is a redirect-following context that never names any of
// those headers. Three things have to be true together for the context to be one.
// A `Location` value is read, which is what makes the code a redirect *follower*
// rather than a redirect *emitter*. A request-options object is being assembled
// with a `headers` property, which is the copy itself. And that same object
// carries a redirect budget -- `follow`, `counter`, `maxRedirects`,
// `redirectCount` and the rest of the spellings clients give it -- which is the
// property that separates a client building the *next* hop from a caller or a
// test merely reading a 3xx response: RFC 7231 requires a follower to bound the
// chain, so every implementation of one has such a field and nothing else does.
// A follower that handles the hop change at all names at least one of the
// credential headers -- deleting it, listing it in a set to strip, or comparing
// against it -- so the absence of every spelling of them is what separates a
// client that considered the boundary from one that did not.
//
// The negation is deliberately on the header names and not on the host
// comparison a fix pairs them with. The comparison has no vocabulary of its own:
// `hostname`, `origin` and `host` are read for a dozen unrelated reasons in the
// same code, and the helper that performs it is named freely. The header names
// are fixed by the HTTP specifications that define them, so they are the part any
// independent implementation of this control has to spell -- as a quoted name in
// a list to delete, or as a header-name pattern, which is why the same names are
// read off both the literal and the regular-expression tokens.
//
// Residual, in three directions. A follower that drops the whole header set
// rather than the credential subset -- rebuilding the next request's headers from
// nothing -- names no credential header either and is reported; that is rare
// because it also drops `Accept` and `User-Agent`. A follower that keeps its strip
// list in a module-level constant is reported too, because a function context
// carries the tokens of the functions nested inside it and not those of the module
// around it, so the names are out of reach from the hop-building function. And a
// function context inheriting its nested functions' tokens means one redirect
// handler nested in a promise executor nested in the client's entry point reports
// once per level rather than once.
binding jsRedirectFollowKeepsCredentialHeaders {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and containsAny(node.context.literal, ["Location", "location"])
    and containsAny(node.context.prop, ["headers="])
    and containsAny(node.context.prop, ["follow=", "counter=", "maxRedirects=", "maxRedirect=", "redirectCount=", "redirectsFollowed=", "maxRedirs="])
    and not containsAny(node.context.literal, ["authorization", "Authorization", "AUTHORIZATION", "cookie", "Cookie", "Cookie2", "www-authenticate", "WWW-Authenticate", "Www-Authenticate", "proxy-authorization", "Proxy-Authorization"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.regex, ["authorization", "Authorization", "cookie", "Cookie", "www-authenticate", "WWW-Authenticate"])
  emit issue code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcrossRedirect at node
}
